steamhelper toolchain notes
===========================

What this is
------------
An x86-64 Linux executable, cross-compiled on ppc64le (op4k), run under FEX
via binfmt_misc.  Its job is to dlopen the real Valve
~/.steam/sdk64/steamclient.so -- which our native ppc64le Wine cannot load --
and serve Proton's flat params-struct calls to the guest steamclient64.dll
over a loopback socket.  See ../check-steam-bridge.sh for the boundary it sits
on, and steamhelper.c for what it does with a frame.

Everything below was measured on op4k on 2026-08-17.  Nothing is guessed.


Host inventory
--------------
    $ uname -m
    ppc64le
    $ clang --version
    clang version 22.1.8
    Target: powerpc64le-unknown-linux-gnu
    $ clang -print-targets | grep x86-64
        x86-64      - 64-bit X86: EM64T and AMD64
    $ ld.lld --version
    LLD 22.1.8 (compatible with GNU linkers)
    $ ld --version | head -1
    GNU ld (GNU Binutils) 2.46.1

The clang install is already a cross compiler -- LLVM ships every backend in
the one binary.  Only the linker and the sysroot need arranging.

Sysroot: the FEX rootfs, unpacked as a plain directory tree at
~/.local/share/fex-emu/RootFS/ArchLinux (there is also an ArchLinux.sqsh, but
the extracted tree is what we point --sysroot at).  Verified present:

    usr/include/stdio.h            usr/include/dlfcn.h
    usr/lib/crt1.o  Scrt1.o  crti.o  crtn.o
    usr/lib/libc.so  libc.so.6  libdl.so.2  libm.so.6  libpthread.so.0
    lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2

usr/lib/libc.so is a GNU ld script with absolute GROUP( /usr/lib/... ) paths.
LLD applies the sysroot prefix to absolute paths in linker scripts that were
themselves found under the sysroot, so this resolves with no extra flags.


The working command line
------------------------
    SYSROOT=$HOME/.local/share/fex-emu/RootFS/ArchLinux

    clang -target x86_64-linux-gnu --sysroot="$SYSROOT" -fuse-ld=lld \
          -std=c99 -D_GNU_SOURCE -O2 -Wall -Wextra \
          steamhelper.c -o steamhelper -ldl

That is exactly what build-helper.sh runs.  Result:

    $ file steamhelper
    steamhelper: ELF 64-bit LSB pie executable, x86-64, version 1 (SYSV),
    dynamically linked, interpreter /lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2,
    for GNU/Linux 4.4.0, BuildID[sha1]=..., not stripped
    $ ls -l steamhelper
    -rwxr-xr-x 1 daedalao daedalao 12568 ... steamhelper

The /lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2 interpreter path is resolved by FEX inside its
own rootfs, not against the ppc64le host filesystem, so the binary needs no
rpath, no -Wl,-dynamic-linker, and no launcher wrapper.

-ldl is a no-op on this glibc (dlopen/dlsym live in libc now).  It is kept
because it costs nothing and keeps the link working against older sysroots.


What did NOT work
-----------------
1. Default linker.  Dropping -fuse-ld=lld drives the host /usr/bin/ld, which
   is a ppc-only binutils build:

       /usr/bin/ld: unrecognised emulation mode: elf_x86_64
       Supported emulations: elf64lppc elf32lppc elf32lppclinux elf64ppc
                             elf32ppc elf32ppclinux elf32lppcsim elf32ppcsim

   There is no x86 emulation in it at all, so -fuse-ld=lld is mandatory.
   LLD infers x86-64 from the input objects; no -m flag needed.

2. No --sysroot.  clang falls back to the host ppc64le /usr/include and then
   multilib-probes for a 32-bit stub header:

       In file included from /usr/include/stdio.h:28:
       /usr/include/gnu/stubs.h:8:11: fatal error: 'gnu/stubs-32.h' file not found

3. -static.  Builds clean, and a static hello-world does run under FEX, so it
   looks like the simpler option.  It is not.  glibc cannot dlopen from a
   fully static image -- there is no dynamic symbol table for the loaded
   library's own glibc references to bind against.  Measured with this exact
   source:

       $ clang -target x86_64-linux-gnu --sysroot=$SYSROOT -fuse-ld=lld \
               -std=c99 -D_GNU_SOURCE -O2 -static steamhelper.c -o hstatic -ldl
       (no diagnostics)
       $ ./hstatic --probe
       helper: uname machine = x86_64
       helper: pointer size = 8
       helper: steamclient = /home/daedalao/.steam/sdk64/steamclient.so
       Segmentation fault (core dumped)          -> exit 139, inside dlopen

   It does not even fail politely.  Dynamic linking against the rootfs loader
   is the correct and simpler answer.


Running it
----------
x86-64 ELF is registered with binfmt_misc:

    /proc/sys/fs/binfmt_misc/FEX-x86_64
      enabled
      interpreter /home/daedalao/projects/fex-emu-ppc64le/src/build-smc/Bin/FEX
      flags: POCF

so the helper is exec'd like any other program -- no `FEX ./steamhelper`
prefix.  This matters: it means native ppc64le Wine can posix_spawn the helper
directly.

    ./steamhelper --probe
    ./steamhelper --serve [--port N] [-v]

STEAMCLIENT_SO overrides the library path; otherwise it is
$HOME/.steam/sdk64/steamclient.so resolved at runtime.

--probe exits 0 only if all seven exports resolved, 1 otherwise (also 1 if
dlopen itself failed).  It dlsyms and never calls, so it cannot disturb a
running Steam client.

--serve binds 127.0.0.1 (loopback only), prints its port on stdout as
"helper: listening on 127.0.0.1:<port>" and flushes, then accepts clients until
killed.  --port 0, the default, asks the kernel for a free one: whoever starts
the helper reads that line and puts the address in the game's
STEAM_BRIDGE_ADDR.  ppc64le/steamtool/proton does exactly that.

An earlier revision of this file documented an --echo mode.  That was the
transport spike and is gone; --serve replaced it.


Measured probe transcript
-------------------------
    $ ./steamhelper --probe
    helper: uname machine = x86_64
    helper: pointer size = 8
    helper: steamclient = /home/daedalao/.steam/sdk64/steamclient.so
    helper: dlopen ok, handle = 0x39a80680060
    export CreateInterface = 0x3fff4122e020
    export Steam_BGetCallback = 0x3fff405afd60
    export Steam_GetAPICallResult = 0x3fff405afde0
    export Steam_FreeLastCallback = 0x3fff405afce0
    export Steam_ReleaseThreadLocalMemory = 0x3fff405af3b0
    export Steam_IsKnownInterface = 0x3fff40238ca0
    export Steam_NotifyMissingInterface = 0x3fff40238d00
    exit 0

All seven exports present in the shipping steamclient.so.  --probe only
dlsyms -- CreateInterface is deliberately NOT called, because it can try to
connect to or spawn the Steam client, and a gate layer must not be able to
disturb one.


Startup cost (matters for the real design)
------------------------------------------
    FEX startup only (usage path, no dlopen)   real 0.104s
    full --probe (FEX + dlopen of 46MB .so)    real 0.567s  user 0.502s
    --probe again, warm page cache             real 0.562s  user 0.498s

The ~460ms dlopen cost does not shrink on a warm run and is almost entirely
user time: it is FEX JIT-translating steamclient.so's init path, not disk I/O.

=> The helper is a long-lived daemon, started once per launch by
   ppc64le/steamtool/proton and stopped when the game exits.  Spawning it per
   Steamworks call would cost half a second every time.


Round-trip cost (measured through the whole chain)
--------------------------------------------------
2000 calls of the marshaller self-test, guest steamclient64.dll -> ws2_32 ->
native Wine -> loopback -> helper -> back, timed inside the guest with
GetTickCount:

    bridge: repeat_count=2000
    bridge: repeat_ms=1016

    => 0.5 ms per Steamworks call.

That is the number the design has to be judged on: a game polling
Steam_BGetCallback a few times per frame at 60fps spends a few percent of a
16ms budget in the bridge.  It is usable, and it is the obvious thing to
attack first if it ever is not (batching the callback poll, or a shared-memory
ring instead of a socket).


Other findings relevant to a real helper
----------------------------------------
- FEX handles AF_UNIX SOCK_STREAM correctly: socket/bind/listen/accept and
  bidirectional transfer all work between a FEX-run x86-64 process and a
  native ppc64le peer.  A 100003-byte frame round-tripped byte-for-byte, so
  partial read/write across the emulation boundary is handled properly (the
  loops in steamhelper.c are still required -- a single read() does not return
  the whole frame).
- uname() reports x86_64 inside FEX, so guest code that sniffs the machine
  type is satisfied.
- dlopen of the 46MB library works and is not memory-pathological.
- Symbol addresses land around 0x3fff4..., i.e. FEX maps the guest library
  high in the address space.  Any pointer we hand back to ppc64le Wine must be
  treated as an opaque 64-bit token belonging to the helper's address space --
  never dereferenced on the Wine side.
